\details{
\strong{Method}

With \eqn{A} the matrix of squared distances and \eqn{J = I - \frac{1}{n}\mathbf{1}\mathbf{1}^T}
the centering matrix, PCoA finds the eigenvalues \eqn{\lambda_i} and unit
eigenvectors \eqn{u_i} of \eqn{B = -\frac{1}{2}JAJ}. Axis \eqn{i}'s
coordinates are \eqn{u_i\sqrt{\lambda_i}}.

\code{cmdscale()}, \code{ape::pcoa()}, and \code{vegan::wcmdscale()} build \eqn{B} in full
(\eqn{n \times n}) and compute all \eqn{n} of its eigenvalues, which takes
time proportional to \eqn{n^3}. \code{pcoa()} finds only the largest \code{k}
eigenvalues, by block Lanczos iteration with thick restarts. That only needs
products of \eqn{B} with a few vectors at a time, which are computed in
parallel straight from \code{dm}, without ever forming \eqn{B}.

\strong{Proportion of variance}

The total variance is \eqn{trace(B) = \frac{1}{n}\sum_{i<j}d_{ij}^2}, the
sum of all \eqn{n} eigenvalues. \code{prop} is each axis's eigenvalue divided by
it: the same as \code{ape::pcoa()}'s \code{Relative_eig} and \code{vegan}'s "Proportion
Explained", the values that \code{phyloseq::plot_ordination()} shows on its axes.

Non-Euclidean distances, such as Bray-Curtis, can give negative eigenvalues.
They still count towards the total, so \code{prop} can sum to more than 1 over
the positive axes. Axes with eigenvalues that aren't positive have no real
coordinates and are dropped with a warning, as \code{cmdscale()} does. So are
axes with eigenvalues at rounding-error level, below
\code{sqrt(.Machine$double.eps)} times the first one, as in \code{vegan::wcmdscale()}.

\strong{Axis signs and reproducibility}

An eigenvector's sign is arbitrary, so different software can return
mirror images of the same axis. \code{pcoa()} flips each axis so that its
largest coordinate in absolute value is positive. Results are identical
from run to run and for any number of \code{cpus}.

\code{k} larger than \eqn{n - 1} (the most axes possible) is reduced to
\eqn{n - 1}.
